gisters  and says `(a0) = undefined' outright.  The module pointer they appear to  want is passed in A3.  So this is not an os9exec gap.  They cannot be rebuilt here either -- <sysglob.h> is Microware's and is the  kernel's internal layout.  Preserved as binaries.  See DOC/README-BUSERR.WILL NOT LOAD -- 0.  FIXED 2026-08-19.  devprc      REBUILT from SRC/devprc and it now loads.  The archived module              had a bad CRC (E_BMCRC 232) AND a corrupt initialised-data              descriptor -- patching the CRC alone turned it into E_BMID,              which proved the body was damaged, not just the checksum.              The EFFO forum-16 copy is byte-identical and equally bad, so              it shipped that way; this is not damage the collection did.              `devprc -h' prints its usage.  `devprc -a' asks F$GPrDBT for              the kernel process table, which os9exec answers without real              data, so the listing itself cannot be shown here.SILENT IN EVERY STAGE -- 34, ALL of them now explained.  Each of these ran without complaint and printednothing, bare, with input, in a session, and asked `-?'.  Several arecertainly correct; the rest want a look.  Correct behaviour, closed:    wisecrack   writes to /PIPE/txtpipe -- nothing appears unless something                is reading that pipe    vtxtcn      writes world's .inc files; needs world's .dat files present    byteflip    byte-swaps a dbz database; needs one handed to it    wysecrack   probes for a Wyse terminal that is not present.  This is the                one program in /dd/CMDS/BROKEN    tex         TeX prompts with `**' and reads standard input; it does    latex       typeset -- see DOC/README-TEX.  `-?' is not its convention    slitex  Explained since, and closed -- 8:    biory       the whole RTF Fortran system needs its run-time library    creadoc     `os9lib' in the module directory.  Every one of these six    for         calls F$Link for it, ge